answer:
Under Annex VII, the provider’s application must include [Annex VII, point 3]:

*   **(a)** the name and address of the provider (and, if lodged by an authorised representative, also their name and address);
*   **(b)** the list of AI systems covered under the same quality management system;
*   **(c)** the technical documentation for each AI system covered under the same quality management system;
*   **(d)** the documentation concerning the quality management system, which must cover all the aspects listed under Article 17 (including, per the provided excerpts, a strategy for regulatory compliance [Art. 17(1)(a)], design control and verification [Art. 17(1)(b)], development and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)], examination, test and validation procedures [Art. 17(1)(d)], technical specifications [Art. 17(1)(e)], the risk management system [Art. 17(1)(g)], and a post-market monitoring system [Art. 17(1)(h)]);
*   **(e)** a description of the procedures in place to ensure that the quality management system remains adequate and effective;
*   **(f)** a written declaration that the same application has not been lodged with any other notified body.

Under Article 92(4), the request for access must state:

*   the **legal basis**;
*   the **purpose and reasons** of the request;
*   the **period within which the access is to be provided**; and
*   the **fines provided for in Article 101** for failure to provide access.

[Art. 92(4)]

Yes. Providers or prospective providers of high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III may test them in real world conditions **outside AI regulatory sandboxes**, provided they meet specific conditions [Art. 60(1)]. Such testing may occur at any time before the system is placed on the market or put into service, either alone or with deployers [Art. 60(2)].

Testing is permitted only where **all** of the following conditions are met [Art. 60(4)]:
- A real-world testing plan has been drawn up and submitted to the market surveillance authority in the Member State where testing will occur [Art. 60(4)(a)];
- The market surveillance authority has approved the testing and plan (or 30 days have passed without response, resulting in tacit approval unless national law requires explicit authorisation) [Art. 60(4)(b)];
- The testing has been registered as required (with specific rules depending on the Annex III category) [Art. 60(4)(c)];
- The predictions, recommendations or decisions of the AI system can be effectively reversed and disregarded [Art. 60(4)(k)].

Providers must also notify the national market surveillance authority of any suspension or termination and of the final outcomes [Art. 60(8)]. This is without prejudice to prohibitions under Article 5 [Art. 60(1)] and any required ethical review [Art. 60(3)].

The **Commission** can update the rules when technology changes by adopting delegated acts to amend:

*   The thresholds, benchmarks and indicators for classifying general-purpose AI models with systemic risk "in light of evolving technological developments" [Art. 51(3)];
*   The criteria in Annex XIII for designating models as presenting systemic risks [Art. 52(4)];
*   Annexes XI and XII (technical documentation and information for downstream providers) "in light of evolving technological developments" [Art. 53(6)].

When you register your use of a high-risk AI system as a deployer, you must provide and keep up to date the following details:

- The name, address and contact details of the deployer;
- The name, address and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer;
- The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider;
- A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27;
- A summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) of this Regulation, where applicable.

[Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5]

This registration obligation applies in particular to deployers that are public authorities, Union institutions, bodies, offices or agencies or persons acting on their behalf, who must register themselves, select the system and register its use in the EU database [Art. 49(3)]. Such deployers must comply with the registration obligations and shall not use the system if it has not been registered by the provider [Art. 26(8)].

This is not legal advice.

For the development and quality of a high-risk AI system, the quality management system must be documented in written policies, procedures and instructions [Art. 17(1)(c)] and must include at least:

- Techniques, procedures and systematic actions for the **development, quality control and quality assurance** of the system [Art. 17(1)(c)];
- Techniques, procedures and systematic actions for the **design, design control and design verification** of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
- **Examination, test and validation procedures** to be carried out before, during and after development, and the frequency with which they have to be carried out [Art. 17(1)(d)];
- Systems and procedures for **data management** covering operations performed before placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 17(1)(f)].

A general-purpose AI model is classified as having systemic risk if it meets either of the conditions in Article 51(1): (a) it has **high impact capabilities** evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks, or (b) the Commission designates it (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel) as having capabilities or an impact **equivalent** to those in point (a), having regard to the criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)].

The specific factors listed in Annex XIII for determining equivalent capabilities or impact are:
*   the **number of parameters** of the model;
*   the **quality or size of the data set** (e.g., measured through tokens);
*   the **amount of computation** used for training (measured in floating point operations, or indicated by estimated cost, time, or energy consumption);
*   the **input and output modalities** (e.g., text-to-text, text-to-image, multi-modality) and state-of-the-art thresholds for high-impact capabilities for each modality, and specific types of inputs/outputs;
*   the **benchmarks and evaluations** of capabilities, including number of tasks without additional training, adaptability, autonomy, scalability, and tools accessed;
*   **high impact on the internal market due to reach**, presumed when made available to at least **10 000 registered business users** established in the Union;
*   the **number of registered end-users** [Annex XIII].

Additionally, a model is **presumed** to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative computation used for its training exceeds **10^25 floating point operations** [Art. 51(2)].

Under the EU AI Act, the Board may, at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, issue recommendations and written opinions on any relevant matters related to the implementation and consistent and effective application of the Regulation, specifically including:

*   the development and application of codes of conduct and codes of practice, as well as the Commission’s guidelines [Art. 66(e)];
*   the evaluation and review of the Regulation pursuant to Article 112 (including serious incident reports under Article 73, the EU database under Article 71, preparation of delegated or implementing acts, and alignments with Union harmonisation legislation in Annex I) [Art. 66(e)];
*   technical specifications or existing standards regarding the requirements in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 66(e)];
*   the use of harmonised standards or common specifications referred to in Articles 40 and 41 [Art. 66(e)];
*   trends such as European global competitiveness in AI, the uptake of AI in the Union, and the development of digital skills [Art. 66(e)];
*   trends on the evolving typology of AI value chains and the resulting implications for accountability [Art. 66(e)];
*   the potential need for amendment to Annex III and for revision of Article 5, taking into account relevant evidence and the latest developments in technology [Art. 66(e)].

Providers may exceptionally process special categories of personal data only when it is **strictly necessary** for bias detection and correction in relation to high-risk AI systems, subject to appropriate safeguards for fundamental rights and freedoms, and only if **all** of the following conditions are met [Art. 10(5)]:

*   the bias detection and correction **cannot be effectively fulfilled** by processing other data, including synthetic or anonymised data [Art. 10(5)(a)];
*   the data are subject to **technical limitations on re-use** and state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures, including pseudonymisation [Art. 10(5)(b)];
*   measures ensure the data are secured and protected with **strict controls and documentation of access**, so that only authorised persons have access with appropriate confidentiality obligations [Art. 10(5)(c)];
*   the data are **not to be transmitted, transferred or otherwise accessed by other parties** [Art. 10(5)(d)];
*   the data are **deleted once the bias has been corrected** or the personal data has reached the end of its retention period, whichever comes first [Art. 10(5)(e)];
*   the records of processing activities include the reasons why processing special categories was strictly necessary and why that objective could not be achieved by processing other data [Art. 10(5)(f)].

The **European Artificial Intelligence Board (the Board)** advises and assists the Commission [Art. 66(e)], [Art. 66(m)], [Art. 66(f)], [Art. 66(c)]. Specifically, the Board:

* issues recommendations and written opinions on implementation and consistent application [Art. 66(e)];
* advises the Commission on international matters on AI [Art. 66(m)];
* supports the Commission in promoting AI literacy and public awareness [Art. 66(f)];
* provides advice on enforcement of rules on general-purpose AI models [Art. 66(c)];
* assists the AI Office in supporting national competent authorities with AI regulatory sandboxes [Art. 66(k)].

In addition, the Commission may appoint **independent experts** to carry out evaluations on its behalf, including experts from the scientific panel established pursuant to Article 68 [Art. 92(2)]. These experts must have up-to-date scientific or technical expertise in AI and meet independence and objectivity criteria [Art. 68(2)].

Before joining, subjects must give freely-given informed consent after being duly informed with concise, clear, relevant, and understandable information regarding [Art. 61(1)]:

*   the nature and objectives of the testing and the possible inconvenience linked to participation [Art. 61(1)(a)];
*   the conditions under which the testing is conducted, including the expected duration of participation [Art. 61(1)(b)];
*   their rights and guarantees, in particular the right to refuse to participate and to withdraw at any time without detriment and without justification [Art. 61(1)(c)];
*   the arrangements for requesting the reversal or disregarding of the AI system’s predictions, recommendations or decisions [Art. 61(1)(d)];
*   the Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing and the contact details of the provider or its legal representative [Art. 61(1)(e)].

The informed consent must also be dated, documented, and a copy given to the subjects or their legal representative [Art. 61(2)].

Providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk must comply with the additional obligations set out in [Art. 55(1)], which apply in addition to the obligations listed in Articles 53 and 54. These are:

*   **Model evaluation and adversarial testing:** perform model evaluation using standardised protocols and tools, including conducting and documenting adversarial testing to identify and mitigate systemic risks [Art. 55(1)(a)];
*   **Risk assessment and mitigation:** assess and mitigate possible systemic risks at Union level, including their sources, stemming from development, placing on the market, or use [Art. 55(1)(b)];
*   **Incident reporting:** keep track of, document, and report without undue delay to the AI Office and, as appropriate, national competent authorities, relevant information about serious incidents and corrective measures [Art. 55(1)(c)];
*   **Cybersecurity:** ensure an adequate level of cybersecurity protection for the model and its physical infrastructure [Art. 55(1)(d)].

In addition, where a model meets the classification condition for high impact capabilities, the provider must notify the Commission without delay and in any event within two weeks [Art. 52(1)].

Under Article 89(2), a downstream provider’s complaint must be **duly reasoned** and indicate at least:

*   the **point of contact** of the provider of the general‑purpose AI model concerned;
*   a **description of the relevant facts**, the **provisions of the Regulation concerned**, and the **reason why** the downstream provider considers that the provider infringed the Regulation;
*   **any other information** that the downstream provider considers relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative. [Art. 89(2)]

The AI Act sets harmonised rules and specific measures in the following areas [Art. 1(2)]:

**Rules:**
*   Harmonised rules for the placing on the market, the putting into service, and the use of AI systems [Art. 1(2)];
*   Prohibitions of certain AI practices [Art. 1(2)];
*   Specific requirements for high-risk AI systems and obligations for operators of such systems [Art. 1(2)];
*   Harmonised transparency rules for certain AI systems [Art. 1(2)];
*   Harmonised rules for the placing on the market of general-purpose AI models [Art. 1(2)];
*   Rules on market monitoring, market surveillance, governance and enforcement [Art. 1(2)].

**Measures:**
*   Measures to support innovation, with a particular focus on SMEs, including start-ups [Art. 1(2)].

The Act follows a risk-based approach that tailors the type and content of rules to the intensity and scope of the risks, prohibiting certain unacceptable AI practices, laying down requirements for high-risk AI systems, and laying down transparency obligations for certain AI systems [Rec

Under Annex IX, you must provide and thereafter keep up to date the following information with regard to testing in real world conditions [Annex IX]:

1. A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX];
2. The name and contact details of the provider or prospective provider and of the deployers involved in the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX];
3. A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and other information necessary for the identification of the system [Annex IX];
4. A summary of the main characteristics of the plan for testing in real world conditions [Annex IX];
5. Information on the suspension or termination of the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX].

For most high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III, this registration is required under Article 60(4)(c) with the information specified in Annex IX [Art. 60(4)(c)].

Under Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6, you must submit and keep up to date the following information regarding AI systems to be registered in accordance with Article 49(2) [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6]:

1. The name, address and contact details of the provider;
2. Where submission is carried out by another person on behalf of the provider, the name, address and contact details of that person;
3. The name, address and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable;
4. The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing the identification and traceability of the AI system;
5. A description of the intended purpose of the AI system;
6. The condition or conditions under Article 6(3) based on which the AI system is considered to be not-high-risk.

Before placing a high-risk AI system on the market, importers must verify that:

* the relevant conformity assessment procedure under Article 43 has been carried out by the provider;
* the provider has drawn up the technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV;
* the system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the EU declaration of conformity under Article 47 and instructions for use;
* the provider has appointed an authorised representative in accordance with Article 22(1). [Art. 23(1)]

Additionally, if the importer has sufficient reason to consider that the system is not in conformity, is falsified, or is accompanied by falsified documentation, it must not place the system on the market until it has been brought into conformity. [Art. 23(2)]

Under Article 93(1), where necessary and appropriate, the Commission may request providers to:

- **take appropriate measures to comply** with the obligations set out in Articles 53 and 54 [Art. 93(1)];
- **implement mitigation measures**, where the evaluation carried out in accordance with Article 92 has given rise to serious and substantiated concern of a systemic risk at Union level [Art. 93(1)];
- **restrict the making available on the market, withdraw or recall the model** [Art. 93(1)].

Under the AI Act, “law enforcement” means activities carried out by law enforcement authorities or on their behalf for:

- the prevention, investigation, detection or prosecution of criminal offences;
- the execution of criminal penalties;
- safeguarding against and preventing threats to public security. [Art. 3(46)]

A “serious incident” is an incident or malfunctioning of an AI system that directly or indirectly leads to any of the following [Art. 3(49)]:

* the death of a person, or serious harm to a person’s health [Art. 3(49)(a)];
* a serious and irreversible disruption of the management or operation of critical infrastructure [Art. 3(49)(b)];
* the infringement of obligations under Union law intended to protect fundamental rights [Art. 3(49)(c)];
* serious harm to property or the environment [Art. 3(49)(d)].

A notifying authority is the national authority responsible for setting up and carrying out the necessary procedures for the **assessment, designation and notification of conformity assessment bodies and for their monitoring** [Art. 3(19)], [Art. 28(1)]. Each Member State must designate or establish at least one such authority, and the procedures shall be developed in cooperation between the notifying authorities of all Member States [Art. 28(1)].

In carrying out these duties, notifying authorities must:

*   Notify the Commission and the other Member States of each conformity assessment body using the electronic notification tool developed and managed by the Commission [Art. 30(2)];
*   Where a notification is not based on an accreditation certificate, provide the Commission and other Member States with documentary evidence attesting to the competence of the conformity assessment body and the arrangements for its regular monitoring [Art. 30(3)];
*   Be organised and operated so that no conflict of interest arises with conformity assessment bodies and that objectivity and impartiality are safeguarded [Art. 28(3)];
*   Ensure that decisions relating to notification are taken by competent persons different from those who carried out the assessment of those bodies [Art. 28(4)];
*   Conduct assessment, designation, notification and monitoring activities, for which notified bodies must make available and submit all relevant documentation upon request [Art. 34(3)];
*   Provide the Commission, on request, with all relevant information relating to the notification or the maintenance of the competence of the notified body [Art. 37(2)].

Generally prohibited unless strictly necessary and all specific conditions are met. For the purpose of finding a suspect, the use may fall under the exception for “the localisation or identification of a person suspected of having committed a criminal offence … for offences … punishable … by a custodial sentence … for a maximum period of at least four years” [Art. 5(1)(h)(iii)], but only if the system is used “only to confirm the identity of the specifically targeted individual” [Art. 5(2)].

The deployment must additionally:
* take into account the seriousness, probability and scale of harm and consequences for rights and freedoms [Art. 5(2)];
* comply with necessary and proportionate safeguards regarding temporal, geographic and personal limitations under national law [Art. 5(2)];
* be preceded by a fundamental rights impact assessment and registration in the EU database (unless urgency applies, with registration completed without undue delay) [Art. 5(2)];
* obtain prior authorisation from a judicial authority or binding independent administrative authority (or, in duly justified urgency, request authorisation within 24 hours; if rejected, stop immediately and delete data) [Art. 5(3)];
* be necessary and proportionate, limited to what is strictly necessary in time, geography and personal scope, and no adverse legal effect may be based solely on the system’s output [Art. 5(3)];
* be notified to the relevant market surveillance authority and national data protection authority [Art. 5(4)].

High-risk AI systems must be designed and developed so they can be **effectively overseen by natural persons** during use [Art. 14(1)]. Human oversight must aim to **prevent or minimise risks to health, safety or fundamental rights** that may emerge when the system is used as intended or under reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 14(2)].

The oversight measures must be **commensurate with the risks, level of autonomy and context of use**, and ensured through either (or both) measures built into the system by the provider, or measures identified by the provider for the deployer to implement [Art. 14(3)].

The system must enable assigned natural persons to [Art. 14(4)]:
- understand the system’s capacities and limitations and monitor its operation, including detecting anomalies and unexpected performance;
- remain aware of **automation bias** (over-reliance on the output);
- correctly interpret the output using available tools and methods;
- decide not to use the system, or to disregard, override or reverse its output; and
- intervene or interrupt the system via a ‘stop’ button or similar safe-halt procedure.

For high-risk AI systems listed in **Annex III, point 1(a)** (biometric identification), no action or decision may be taken by the deployer based on the system’s identification unless it has been **separately verified and confirmed by at least two natural persons** with the necessary competence, training and authority; this two-person rule does not apply to law enforcement, migration, border control or asylum systems where Union or national law deems it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

Deployers must **assign human oversight to natural persons** who have the necessary competence, training, authority and support [Art. 26(2)], and the instructions for use must describe the human oversight measures and technical tools facilitating output interpretation [Art. 13(3)(d)].

High-risk AI systems must be accompanied by instructions for use in an appropriate digital format or otherwise that are "concise, complete, correct and clear" and "relevant, accessible and comprehensible to deployers" [Art. 13(2)].

These instructions must contain at least:

*   The identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative [Art. 13(3)(a)];
*   The characteristics, capabilities and limitations of performance, including: intended purpose; expected accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity levels and metrics and any known circumstances affecting them; known or foreseeable circumstances that may lead to risks to health, safety or fundamental rights; technical capabilities to explain outputs; performance regarding specific persons or groups; specifications for input data; and information to enable deployers to interpret and use the output appropriately [Art. 13(3)(b)];
*   Any pre-determined changes to the system and its performance from the initial conformity assessment [Art. 13(3)(c)];
*   The human oversight measures, including technical measures to facilitate interpretation of outputs [Art. 13(3)(d)];
*   The computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance and care measures (including frequency and software updates) [Art. 13(3)(e)];
*   Where relevant, a description of mechanisms allowing deployers to collect, store and interpret logs [Art. 13(3)(f)].

Additionally, the levels of accuracy and relevant accuracy metrics must be declared in the accompanying instructions [Art. 15(3)].

Notified bodies must satisfy requirements concerning their legal form, organisational structure, impartiality, resources, personnel competence, and participation in standardisation. Where they demonstrate conformity with harmonised standards published in the Official Journal of the European Union, they are presumed to comply with the Article 31 requirements in so far as those standards cover them [Art. 32].

Specifically, to perform their duties they must:

- Be established under the national law of a Member State and have legal personality [Art. 31(1)];
- Satisfy organisational, quality management, resources and process requirements, as well as suitable cybersecurity requirements [Art. 31(2)];
- Ensure their organisational structure, allocation of responsibilities, reporting lines and operation ensure confidence in their performance and in the results of conformity assessment activities [Art. 31(3)];
- Be organised and operated to safeguard independence, objectivity and impartiality, and document and implement structures and procedures to promote impartiality throughout their organisation, personnel and assessment activities [Art. 31(6)];
- Have permanent availability of sufficient administrative, technical, legal and scientific personnel possessing experience and knowledge relating to the relevant types of AI systems, data, data computing and the requirements of Section 2, and sufficient internal competences to evaluate tasks conducted by external parties [Art. 31(11)];
- Participate in coordination activities referred to in Article 38, and take part directly, or be represented in, European standardisation organisations, or ensure they are aware and up to date regarding relevant standards [Art. 31(12)].
